(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is first-line drug therapy for symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Adenosine","Beta blocker","Intravenous magnesium","Digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is treated first with a beta blocker such as metoprolol or carvedilol. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What separates pulseless electrical activity from asystole?","choices":["An organized rhythm, no pulse","A flat line, no activity","Chaotic waves, no pulse","A wide rhythm with a pulse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity shows an electrocardiogram on which you would expect a pulse, such as normal sinus rhythm, but no perfusing beats; asystole shows nothing. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which premature ventricular contraction burden puts a patient in the high-risk group?","choices":["Any single ectopic","Over 0.5% of beats","Over 1% of beats","Over 15% of beats"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high burden, over 15% of beats or 15,000 a day, is one of the high-risk features, with premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, structural or conduction disease and syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Low calcium, high sodium","High phosphate, low chloride","Low potassium, low magnesium","High potassium, low sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, which symptom is associated with an increased risk of sudden death?","choices":["Syncope","Fatigue","Palpitations","Ankle swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is a particularly important symptom in complete heart block because it is associated with an increased risk of sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous lidocaine alone","Observation on a monitor","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even if the patient looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"In an elderly patient, which cause tops the list for both Mobitz type II and complete atrioventricular block?","choices":["Recent Lyme infection","Inherited long QT","His-Purkinje degeneration","Increased vagal tone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acquired disease of the His-Purkinje and conduction system, especially degenerative change from old age with chronic hypertension and diabetes, is the most common cause of both. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 13, 18"},{"q":"Which procedure is a named cause of Mobitz type I block?","choices":["Pericardiocentesis","Transthoracic echocardiography","Cardiac ablation","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac ablation is listed among the causes of Mobitz type I block, along with increased vagal tone, atrioventricular nodal blocking drugs, Lyme carditis and ischemia after infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which is the most common cause of first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Overactive vagal tone","Age-related conduction degeneration","Lyme disease of the heart","Atrioventricular node ischemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause: the pathway wears out, so the impulse takes longer to cross the atrioventricular node. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which two arrest rhythms are shockable?","choices":["Fibrillation, pulseless tachycardia","Asystole, fibrillation","Pulseless electrical, asystole","Tachycardia, pulseless electrical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia are treated with defibrillation; asystole and pulseless electrical activity are unshockable. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slides 50, 55"},{"q":"For an adult with rare premature ventricular contractions, a normal heart and no symptoms, which care strategy fits?","choices":["Implanted defibrillator","Reassurance, avoid triggers","Beta blocker for life","Ablation this month"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low-risk adults with a low burden (under 1% or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms are reassured and helped to eliminate triggers. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which chronic pulmonary vascular condition is a named cause of right bundle branch block?","choices":["Pulmonary hypertension","Pleural effusion","Obstructive sleep apnea","Solitary pulmonary nodule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is a named cause of right bundle branch block, along with age-related degeneration, ischemic heart disease, cardiomyopathies and inflammatory or infiltrative disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is a Stokes-Adams attack?","choices":["Palpitations felt pounding in the neck","Exertional chest pain relieved by rest","Seizure caused by low blood glucose","Sudden faint from low brain blood flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Stokes-Adams attack is a sudden fainting spell or seizure from a rapid, temporary drop in blood flow to the brain, a complication of Mobitz type II block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which chronic condition is a named cause of left bundle branch block?","choices":["Chronic pulmonary hypertension","Chronic hepatitis","Chronic migraine","Chronic hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypertension heads the list of causes of left bundle branch block, followed by chronic ischemic heart disease, dilated cardiomyopathy, aortic stenosis and Lenègre-Lev disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which parasitic infection is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Giardiasis","Chagas disease","Malaria","Toxoplasmosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease is listed among the causes of complete block, with Lyme carditis, bacterial endocarditis with an abscess and inflammatory or infiltrative disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For an elderly patient with Mobitz type II block found incidentally and causing no symptoms, which plan is appropriate?","choices":["Start a beta blocker","Permanent pacemaker","Reassure and observe","Recheck in a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression to complete block, and the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which history places a patient with premature ventricular contractions in the high-risk group?","choices":["Age under 30","Occasional anxiety","Prior syncope","Daily caffeine use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-risk features are a burden over 15%, premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, pre-existing structural or conduction disease and a history of syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why are amiodarone and procainamide avoided in torsades de pointes?","choices":["They prolong the QT interval","They lower serum magnesium","They shorten the PR interval","They cause severe hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone and procainamide prolong the QT interval, which would worsen the substrate of torsades; magnesium is the treatment instead. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is ventricular bigeminy?","choices":["Every third beat is ectopic","PR lengthens, then drops","Three ectopics in a row","Every other beat is ectopic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When every other beat is an ectopic beat the pattern is bigeminy; when every third beat is ectopic it is trigeminy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern defines Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Long constant PR, no dropped beats","Constant PR with random dropped QRS","PR lengthens until a QRS drops","P waves unrelated to the QRS"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II looks regular apart from random missing QRS complexes; the PR interval of the conducted beats stays constant at 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which is the management of asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Lifelong oral amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the underlying disease","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is managed by optimizing treatment of the underlying cardiac comorbidities. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why are beta blockers avoided in drug-induced torsades de pointes?","choices":["They cause hypokalemia","They shorten the QT too much","They interact with magnesium","Bradycardia lengthens the QT"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers cause bradycardia and lengthen the R to R interval, which indirectly prolongs the QT and worsens acquired torsades. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which statement about shocking asystole and pulseless electrical activity is accurate?","choices":["Both need defibrillation","Shock if the line is flat","Shock if the rhythm is organized","Both are unshockable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity and asystole are unshockable; chest compressions start immediately, with epinephrine and early intubation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which external device detects and shocks fatal ventricular arrhythmias while worn on the body?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing pads","Wearable defibrillator vest","Implantable loop recorder","Ambulatory Holter monitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There is a wearable (external) cardioverter defibrillator, worn as a vest, that detects fatal ventricular arrhythmias and delivers a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Above which PR interval do symptoms of first-degree atrioventricular block usually begin to appear?","choices":["0.12 seconds","0.20 seconds","0.3 seconds","0.6 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The vast majority of first-degree blocks are asymptomatic; symptoms such as fatigue, shortness of breath, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of asystole and pulseless electrical activity?","choices":["Excellent with shocks","Good if found early","Poor; usually end-of-life","Better than fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity carry a poor prognosis and are usually end-of-life rhythms; ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation have the best prognosis of the arrest rhythms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death?","choices":["An accessory pathway","Coronary artery disease","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Long QT syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery disease is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death, even in patients without a history of myocardial infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When ventricular fibrillation stays refractory to shocks after an antiarrhythmic, which drug may be considered?","choices":["Verapamil","Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is considered for ventricular fibrillation that remains refractory to shocks after amiodarone. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Once stabilized after unstable ventricular tachycardia, what do most patients need?","choices":["An implantable defibrillator","A permanent pacemaker","Lifelong oral magnesium","No further treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death from ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"}]};
